Change per passage (what the representation did):
   1   2.5  ~~
          Socrates depicts chained cave-prisoners who can perceive only shadows and echoes,
          establishing them as like ourselves in enlightenment.
   2 12.75  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates shows that release from illusion begins with painful glare, confusion, and
          resistance, as the prisoner clings to familiar shadows before being dazzled by greater
          realities up to the sun.
   3 12.75  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates traces the freed prisoner's gradual ascent from shadows and reflections to
          the sun itself as cause of the visible world, leaving him to pity his former den and
          despise its shadow-honours.
   4 47.25  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates shows the returning philosopher blinded, ridiculed, and threatened with
          death, then explicitly decodes the cave as the visible world and crowns the ascent
          with the Idea of the Good.
   5  30.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates excuses the enlightened philosopher's unwillingness to return and ridiculous
          blindness in human affairs, then teaches that dazzlement comes from either entering
          light or darkness and should be diagnosed rather than mocked.
   6  20.5  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates rejects teachers who claim to insert knowledge into the soul, arguing that
          education is instead the art of turning the soul's innate power from becoming toward
          being and the Good.
   7  18.5  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates argues that keen natures freed from sensual drag could see truth sharply, so
          the founders must compel those who reach the Good to descend again and govern.
   8  20.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates answers Glaucon's charge of injustice by arguing that educated philosophers
          owe ruling service to the whole State, whose reluctant rulers will govern best.
   9  19.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates concludes that just philosophic pupils will accept ruling as a stern
          necessity, and only rulers given a better life of philosophy and rich in virtue can
          keep the State well-ordered.
Constructed meaning c02_1 (passage 3, inferred): Liberation is not instant unchaining but painful, staged retraining of vision culminating in grasp of the cause of the visible, which alone makes the former prison appear pitiable and its honours worthless.
    built from: Painful forced liberation (provides initial freeing as pain and dazzlement
    rather than immediate clarity); Shadows seem truer (shows persisting epistemic loyalty to
    old illusions that blocks immediate seeing); Stages of Upper Vision (provides gradual
    habituation through shadows to reflections to things that makes higher vision tolerable);
    Sun as World Guardian (provides culminating causal insight that the sun governs visible
    things); Pity for Cave Fellows (registers retrospective revaluation of former dwelling as
    pitiable); Contempt for Shadow Honours (registers active rejection of the cave value-system
    of shadow-prediction)
Constructed meaning c03_1 (passage 4, inferred): The philosopher's return inverts the ascent's value: competence in truth appears within the cave as blindness, ridicule, and a capital threat, so enlightenment is socially self-defeating on cave terms.
    built from: Contempt for Shadow Honours (provides the philosopher's prior devaluation of
    shadow-prediction honours as worthless); Returnee blinded by darkness (provides the
    physiological reversal where sun-adapted eyes fail in darkness); Ridiculed shadow competitor
    (provides the prisoners' social verdict that ascent destroyed sight); Death threat to
    liberator (escalates dismissal into lethal defence of the cave against liberation)
Constructed meaning c03_2 (passage 4, explicit): The cave's light-hierarchy is demoted one level: the sun that seemed to guard and cause all visibles is itself an effect of the Idea of the Good, making the visible world doubly derivative.
    built from: Sun as World Guardian (establishes the sun's apparent ultimacy as giver of
    seasons and guardian of visibles); Cave to intellect mapping (fixes prison, fire and ascent
    as images for visible world, sun and soul's ascent to intellect, placing the sun inside the
    lower order); Good as ultimate source (posits the Idea of the Good seen last as universal
    author of beauty, right, light and truth)
Constructed meaning c04_1 (passage 5, explicit): Confusion about truth in both directions, turning away from glare when going into light and stumbling in darkness when coming out of light, is the same excusable dazzle and must be diagnosed by direction rather than mocked.
    built from: Retreat from glaring light (provides the ascending case where glare drives
    turning back to familiar visibles); Returner's courtroom blindness (provides the descending
    case where return to darkness produces failure that looks ridiculous); Two kinds of
    blindness (supplies the general principle that bewilderment has two opposite causes for eye
    and mind); Suspend mockery and diagnose (supplies the normative consequence to suspend
    laughter and diagnose direction first)
Constructed meaning c04_2 (passage 5, explicit): The philosopher's unwillingness to return is not arrogance but the happiness of dwelling in light, to be distinguished from the pity owed to the soul still blinded while ascending.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (states unwillingness to descend as longing to
    dwell above after beatific vision); Pity for Cave Fellows (provides the earlier fellow-
    feeling that congratulates self-change while pitying those left behind); Happy descent
    versus pity (refines pity into an asymmetrical verdict counting descending dazzle happy and
    ascending dazzle pitiable)
Constructed meaning c05_1 (passage 6, inferred): The painful compulsion in the cave is the turning of an already-seeing power from becoming toward being, so education is not putting sight into blind eyes but the art of redirecting existing sight toward the Good.
    built from: Chained Underground Prisoners (provides the initial fixed gaze at shadows as
    misdirection rather than blindness); Painful forced liberation (provides the painful
    compulsory turning that is to be explained); Innate power of learning (establishes that the
    capacity for learning already exists in the soul); Art of easy conversion (defines true
    education as reorienting existing sight rather than implanting it); Whole-soul turning
    toward being (specifies the required movement as whole-soul turn from becoming to being for
    vision of the Good)
Constructed meaning c06_1 (passage 7, explicit): Fitness to rule requires both possession of truth and compelled re-entry into the cave, making ignorant fixation on shadows below and contemplative withdrawal above symmetric disqualifications cured only by forced descent.
    built from: Mistaking Shadows for Reality (provides the lower pole of unfitness: ignorance
    that mistakes shadows for reality); Philosopher unwilling to descend (provides the upper
    pole of unfitness: attainment of vision that refuses descent to human affairs); Neither
    Extreme Can Rule (states the joint political judgment that neither pole can supply able
    ministers); Compelled Descent to Govern (provides the imposed remedy that joins truth to
    rule by forcing return to shared labour)
Constructed meaning c06_2 (passage 7, inferred): The same native keenness that makes unconverted cleverness mischievous in proportion to its sharpness would see truth with equal sharpness once cut free from sensual drag, so philosophical capacity is direction-dependent rather than self-guiding.
    built from: Clever rogue's misdirected sight (shows that unconverted keenness serves evil in
    proportion to cleverness); Leaden Weights of Pleasure (identifies sensual pleasures as the
    downward drag that fixes that direction); Freed Faculty Sees Truth (shows that the same
    faculty freed from that drag would see truth with equal keenness)
Constructed meaning c07_1 (passage 8, explicit): Unwillingness to descend, once mere longing to remain above, becomes the credential of the best rulers whose compelled service for the happiness of the whole State is just.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (supplies the reluctant longing to dwell above
    that resists descent); Reluctant Rulers Govern Best (revalues that reluctance as the mark of
    the best and most quietly governed rule); Happiness Of Whole State (supplies the happiness
    of the whole State as the end that overrides class interest); Compelling Philosophers Is
    Just (delivers the verdict that compelling such philosophers to care for others is not
    unjust)
Constructed meaning c07_2 (passage 8, inferred): The darkness-blindness that made the returner ridiculous becomes learnable night-vision that fits hive-bred philosophers to govern the underground abode.
    built from: Returnee blinded by darkness (provides sudden blindness on being replaced in the
    old situation); Descent To Underground Abode (orders repeated descent to learn to see in the
    dark below); Philosophers As Hive Rulers (identifies these returners as bred and educated to
    be rulers of the hive)
Constructed meaning c08_1 (passage 9, explicit): Compelled descent ceases to be mere external coercion because assurance of predominant shared heavenly life turns lingering reluctance to dwell above into willing acceptance of ruling as stern necessity.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (establishes initial reluctance to leave
    contemplation for human affairs); Compelled Descent to Govern (imposes the external
    requirement to descend and share labours and honours); Pupils' Shared Heavenly Life
    (provides the compensating assurance of predominant shared time in heavenly light while
    taking turns); Ruling as Stern Necessity (records the resulting acceptance of office as
    stern necessity rather than refusal)
Constructed meaning c08_2 (passage 9, explicit): Only rulers enriched in virtue and wisdom by the better life of true philosophy can keep the State well-ordered because looking down on political ambition immunizes them against ruinous fighting for office.
    built from: Better Life For Rulers (states that a better life than ruling must be contrived
    to produce a well-ordered State); Philosophy Above Political Ambition (identifies that
    better life as true philosophy alone looking down on political ambition); Riches In Virtue
    Wisdom (specifies the wealth of that life as virtue and wisdom rather than silver and gold);
    Office Fighting Ruins State (states the ruinous fighting about office that this disdain for
    ambition prevents)

